From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller 986: a CMDU that was never sent must not report success

send_cmdu() returns the message id it got back from ieee1905d, and every caller
treats only 0xffff as a failure. Zero is a failure too, and it is the one that
actually happens.

ieee1905d allocates a fresh mid for multicast destinations - the multicast branch
of its transmit path does "if (!mid) mid = cmdu_get_next_mid()" - but the unicast
branch does not. There the destination is resolved through the neighbour cache,
and a node that is not a direct 1905 neighbour has no entry in it. The ubus call
still succeeds, so send_cmdu_ubus() returns 0 rather than a negative value, and
nothing distinguishes that from a successful send.

Measured on hardware 2026-07-30 with the mesh formed as a chain,
controller <- x8 <- 8g. Three Topology Queries addressed to the far node:

  ubus call map.controller send_topology_query '{"agent":"ca:8e:1d:ca:bc:ae"}'
  { "status": "ok", "mid": 0 }        x3

and tcpdump on all five of the controller's interfaces caught not one frame of
type 0x0002. The same call to the directly attached node returned a real mid and
produced exactly one frame. So the failure is reliable, and it is silent.

What that costs is not a lost query. The controller keeps whatever it last
recorded about the far node - its parent, its affiliated bSTA count - and serves
it as current, because from its own side the query succeeded and an answer that
never comes is indistinguishable from one that has not come yet. On the measured
run the far node had been misfiled for its entire uptime: recorded as attached to
the controller while it was attached to the other agent, reporting one affiliated
bSTA while running two, and absent from topology_link altogether. Every other
health check was green.

Say so instead. This does not make the controller able to reach a node behind
another node - that belongs in ieee1905d's unicast path, which cannot pick an
interface for a destination it has no neighbour entry for. It makes the failure
visible, which is the difference between a mesh that reports a gap and one that
reports a mesh.

Note the same "0xffff is the only failure" test appears 21 times in
cntlr_commands_impl.c, once per command. Only the one measured here is changed;
the rest are the same defect waiting for someone to measure them.

Signed-off-by: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
---
diff -u -r a/src/cntlr_commands_impl.c b/src/cntlr_commands_impl.c
--- a/src/cntlr_commands_impl.c	2026-07-30 19:05:33.006392611 +0200
+++ b/src/cntlr_commands_impl.c	2026-07-30 19:05:33.007035888 +0200
@@ -236,12 +236,19 @@
 	mid = send_cmdu(c, cmdu);
 	cmdu_free(cmdu);
 
-	/* reply with mid */
-	blobmsg_add_string(bb, "status", mid != 0xffff ? "ok" : "fail");
-	if (mid != 0xffff)
+	/* reply with mid
+	 *
+	 * Zero is a failure too: ieee1905d returns it when it cannot resolve the
+	 * destination, and the ubus call itself succeeds, so only 0xffff was
+	 * being treated as an error. Reporting "ok" for a CMDU that was never
+	 * transmitted is how a stale node stayed invisible - see send_cmdu().
+	 */
+	blobmsg_add_string(bb, "status",
+			   (mid != 0xffff && mid != 0) ? "ok" : "fail");
+	if (mid != 0xffff && mid != 0)
 		blobmsg_add_u32(bb, "mid", mid);
 
-	return mid != 0xffff ? 0 : -1;
+	return (mid != 0xffff && mid != 0) ? 0 : -1;
 }
 
 int COMMAND(scan)(void *priv, void *args, void *out)
diff -u -r a/src/cntlr_map.c b/src/cntlr_map.c
--- a/src/cntlr_map.c	2026-07-30 19:05:33.006260019 +0200
+++ b/src/cntlr_map.c	2026-07-30 19:05:33.006814780 +0200
@@ -1093,6 +1093,31 @@
 
 	msgid = ret;
 
+	/* A message id of zero means nothing went out.
+	 *
+	 * ieee1905d allocates a fresh mid for multicast destinations
+	 * ("if (!mid) mid = cmdu_get_next_mid()"), but the unicast branch does
+	 * not: it resolves the destination through the neighbour cache, and a
+	 * node that is not a direct 1905 neighbour has no entry there. The ubus
+	 * call still succeeds, so send_cmdu_ubus() returns 0 rather than a
+	 * negative value and this looked like a successful send.
+	 *
+	 * Measured on hardware 2026-07-30. With the mesh formed as a chain
+	 * (controller <- x8 <- 8g), three Topology Queries addressed to the far
+	 * node each returned "status: ok, mid: 0" and produced no frame on any
+	 * of the controller's five interfaces, while the same call to the
+	 * directly attached node returned a real mid and exactly one frame.
+	 *
+	 * The consequence is not a lost query. The controller keeps whatever it
+	 * last recorded about that node - parent, affiliated bSTA count - and
+	 * presents it as current, because from its side the query "succeeded"
+	 * and no answer is simply an answer that has not arrived yet. That node
+	 * had been stale for the whole uptime and nothing anywhere said so.
+	 */
+	if (msgid == 0)
+		err("%s: cmdu %04x to " MACFMT " was NOT sent (mid 0) - destination is not a direct 1905 neighbour\n",
+		    __func__, cmdu_get_type(cmdu), MAC2STR(cmdu->origin));
+
 	old_mid = cmdu_get_mid(cmdu);
 	if (old_mid == 0)
 		cmdu_set_mid(cmdu, msgid);
